## Why This Is Asked

Real-world IPC, process isolation, and policy enforcement are needed for privacy-critical features. This question tests ability to design a trusted mediator, enforce per-app access, and handle token rotation without destabilizing apps.

## Key Concepts

- Android isolatedProcess and binder IPC via AIDL
- Clipboard interception and privacy filtering
- Hardware-backed Keystore encryption and per-app keys
- Policy-driven redaction and auditing
- Lifecycle and crash resilience

## Code Example

```kotlin
// AIDL and service skeleton (conceptual)
interface IClipboardGuard {
 String getLastClipboard();
 void rotateToken(String appId, String token);
}

class ClipboardGuardService : Binder(), IClipboardGuard {
 // stub: access control and interception logic
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you implement per-app access verification across IPC calls?
- What testing strategy ensures policy correctness and resilience to clipboard noise?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design cross‑protocol data models and practical adapters.

## Key Concepts
- Shared domain model across REST, GraphQL, and gRPC
- Versioned contracts and simple compatibility rules
- Per-protocol adapters and a unified error envelope
- Tenant-aware contract loading for OpenAPI

## Code Example
```yaml
openapi: 3.0.0
info: {title: User service, version: v1}
paths:
 /users/{id}:
 get: { responses: { '200': { description: OK, content: { 'application/json': { schema: { $ref: '#/components/schemas/User' } } }}}}}
components:
 schemas:
 User:
 type: object
 properties:
 id: { type: string }
 name: { type: string }
 email: { type: string }
```
```graphql
type User {
 id: ID!
 name: String!
 email: String!
}
```
```proto
message User {
 string id = 1;
 string name = 2;
 string email = 3;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test cross‑protocol compatibility during CI?
- How would you handle breaking schema changes across REST/GraphQL/gRPC?

## Why This Is Asked This question tests practical STAR storytelling and the ability to translate a real technical constraint into a structured narrative, highlighting risk management, trade-offs, and validation steps in a MongoDB operation. ## Key Concepts - STAR method: Situation, Task, Action, Result - MongoDB replica set upgrades and rolling maintenance - Downtime mitigation and rollback planning - Data integrity verification - Scope negotiation and stakeholder communication ## Code Example ```javascript function formatSTAR(situation, task, action, result) { return `S: ${situation}
T: ${task}
A: ${action}
R: ${result}`; } ``` ## Follow-up Questions - What trade-offs did you consider when choosing rolling upgrades vs. other strategies? - How would this approach adapt to a sharded cluster or multi-region deployment?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests compact, deterministic bit-trickery without loops, showing knowledge of parallel popcount progressions.

## Key Concepts
- Masks for pairwise sums
- Shifts to propagate counts
- Edge-case: 0 yields 0

## Code Example
```javascript
uint32_t popcount32(uint32_t x) {
 x = (x & 0x55555555) + ((x >> 1) & 0x55555555);
 x = (x & 0x33333333) + ((x >> 2) & 0x33333333);
 x = (x & 0x0F0F0F0F) + ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F);
 x = (x & 0x00FF00FF) + ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF);
 x = (x & 0x0000FFFF) + ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF);
 return x & 0x3F;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for 64-bit inputs without 128-bit math?
- How to test exhaustively across 32-bit space?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to derive circular adjacency via rotation, and to implement a 128-bit popcount using SWAR style reductions; ensures understanding of cross-boundary bit relationships.

## Key Concepts

- Circular adjacency using ROTL
- 128-bit representation as two 64-bit halves
- SWAR/popcount with bitwise masks and shifts

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

static inline uint128 rotl128(uint128 x){
 uint128 r;
 r.hi = (x.hi << 1) | (x.lo >> 63);
 r.lo = (x.lo << 1) | (x.hi >> 63);
 return r;
}

static inline unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x){
 uint64_t a = x.hi;
 uint64_t b = x.lo;
 a = a - ((a >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
 a = (a & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((a >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
 a = (a + (a >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
 unsigned int ca = (a * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;

 b = b - ((b >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
 b = (b & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((b >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
 b = (b + (b >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
 unsigned int cb = (b * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;

 return ca + cb;
}
```

Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt to non-power-of-two widths?
- What are performance implications on vectorized lanes?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to perform full-width bit reversal in constant time using SWAR patterns, not per-bit loops.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit composition as hi/lo 64-bit
- SWAR bit-reversal: 1,2,4,8,16,32-bit block swaps
- Final cross-half swap to complete reversal

## Code Example
```javascript
// 64-bit reversal helper
static uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t x){
 x = (x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL | (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) << 1;
 x = (x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL | (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) << 2;
 x = (x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL | (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) << 4;
 x = (x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL | (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8;
 x = (x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL | (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16;
 x = (x >> 32) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL | (x & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL) << 32;
 return x;
}
uint128 reverse128(uint128 x){
 uint128 r;
 r.lo = reverse64(x.hi);
 r.hi = reverse64(x.lo);
 return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to larger widths (256-bit) with minimal extra code?
- What intrinsics or compiler options help optimize this on typical CPUs?

Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to reason about 128-bit values and apply bit-twiddling without branching. This requires cross-half logic and edge-case handling.

Key Concepts
- 128-bit decomposition into hi and lo
- a 64-bit value is a power of two if v & (v-1) == 0 and v != 0
- exactly one half nonzero implies overall power-of-two only if that half is itself a power of two

Code Example

```c
uint8_t isPowerOfTwo128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo) {
 // Exactly one half must be nonzero and that half must be a power of two
 int hiPow = (hi & (hi - 1)) == 0 && hi != 0;
 int loPow = (lo & (lo - 1)) == 0 && lo != 0;
 return ((hi == 0) ^ (lo == 0)) ? (hi ? hiPow : loPow) : 0;
}
```

Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize for a specific CPU architecture (e.g., using intrinsics)?
- How would you extend this to 256-bit values?

