To diagnose CPU frequency scaling issues causing tail latency spikes in a low-latency REST API, follow this structured workflow using default Linux tools: ```bash # 1. Baseline: Check current governors and frequencies for c in /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu*/cpufreq/scaling_governor; do echo "$c: $(cat $c)"; done for c in /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu*/cpufreq/scaling_cur_freq; do echo "$c: $(cat $c)"; done # 2. Monitor real-time frequency changes during load bursts watch -n 0.1 "grep -H . /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu*/cpufreq/scaling_cur_freq" # 3. Identify hot threads and their CPU affinity top -H -p $(pgrep -f your-api-process) # observe thread IDs and CPU usage taskset -c -p <thread-id> # check current affinity # 4. Pin hot threads to dedicated cores for consistent performance taskset -cp 0-3 <pid> # pin main process to cores 0-3 for tid in $(pgrep -f your-api-process); do taskset -cp 0-3 $tid; done # 5. Set performance governor on pinned cores echo performance | sudo tee /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu{0,1,2,3}/cpufreq/scaling_governor # 6. Verify mitigation effectiveness watch -n 0.1 "grep -H . /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu{0,1,2,3}/cpufreq/scaling_cur_freq" curl -w '@curl-format.txt' http://localhost:8080/api/endpoint # monitor latency ``` Create curl-format.txt for detailed timing: ``` time_namelookup: %{time_namelookup}
 time_connect: %{time_connect}
 time_appconnect: %{time_appconnect}
 time_pretransfer: %{time_pretransfer}
 time_redirect: %{time_redirect}
 time_starttransfer: %{time_starttransfer}
 time_total: %{time_total}
``` Expected outputs confirming successful mitigation: - Governor status: `/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu0/cpufreq/scaling_governor: performance` - Stable frequencies: `/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu0/cpufreq/scaling_cur_freq: 3500000` - Improved latency: `time_total: 0.045` (reduced from 100-300ms spikes)

